Plan says memoized DFS, implementation docs clarify not memoized. Not needed.

Now, potential concern: Is the DFS optimization straightforward? Let's design carefully and check if any subtle semantics break.

Current algorithm frames:
- Start frame candidate, edges [], visited {candidate}
- Loop top frame:
  - if node === origin: if edges.length > 0 push frozen path; pop; continue.
  - edges = adjacency[node]; if next >= len pop.
  - take edge, next++
  - if visited.has(edge.parent) continue
  - push child frame with edges [...], visited copy + parent.

With backtracking:
- pathEdges: LineageEdge[] = []
- visited = new Set([candidate])
- stack of {node, edges, next}
- On start, process top:
  - if node === origin: if pathEdges.length > 0 emit frozenPath(scope, pathEdges); then pop and must undo the last edge (remove origin from visited and pop pathEdges) before continuing parent frame. Important: for start candidate==origin, pathEdges empty, visited has candidate, pop; no undo.
  - if exhausted: pop; undo unless stack empty? Each frame except root has an incoming edge in pathEdges. So after pop, if stack.length > 0, remove frame.node from visited and pop pathEdges. Order: pathEdges last edge corresponds to frame edge (child -> node). Root no.
  - else take edge; if visited.has parent skip; else pathEdges.push(edge), visited.add(parent), stack.push(frame for parent).

Emission: when reaching origin, adjacency not explored (we don't expand origin), so we emit and pop. Undo correctly.

