Potential issue with memory: new DFS uses `pathEdges` plus stack frames. On a 10k chain, stack has 10k frames storing node/edges reference/next; pathEdges 10k; visited 10k. O(n). Emission copies 10k. Good.

But there's a subtlety: In current code, `frame.node === origin` check happens even for the root candidate; guarded by edges.length>0. New code should keep that.

Another subtlety: current code copies `visited` before push and adds parent. New single visited must remove only parent when popping child. What if multiple frames contain same node? Visited prevents that, so unique. Good.

What about adjacencyFor result map: child entries created even if all edges? It sets adjacency for every traversed edge only. New nested map creates child entry on first included edge. Same.

What about sorting: current sort comparator compares parent then relation. If duplicate edges were deduped, unique. New same. Sort is stable but no duplicates so deterministic.

