- Con: `graphEdgeIndex` is defined above adjacencyFor in file, easy. But it caches across calls; discovery currently doesn't mutate global cache except this. Memory retention tied to graph (WeakMap) okay. It also computes index even when discoverPaths later finds no path? Well adjacencyFor only called after reachable check. Fine.
- Potential subtle semantic difference: graphEdgeIndex is keyed by edge.child and includes duplicate edges once; identical. No difference.
- Diff is small: replace loop with index traversal. But iteration order of Maps is insertion order of graph.edges, then sorted per child. Final sorted same. Deterministic.
- This approach is arguably best but issue said "Map<parent+relation, edge>" not necessarily.

Wait: `graphEdgeIndex` builds `Map<child, Map<parent, Set<relation>>>`; no edge object stored, but adjacency needs child,parent,relation so can construct. Good.

Potential issue: `graphEdgeIndex` only used for membership. If adjacencyFor uses it, then `discoverPaths` gets a cache entry; subsequent `validatePath` uses same. Great. But if the graph is huge and we only need a small reachable subgraph, building full index costs O(E) which adjacencyFor already scans graph.edges O(E) anyway. Same.

